<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Conrad von Hötzendorf</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf was Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> of the Austro-Hungarian army. He repeatedly urged preventive war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and his aggressive offensive plans in 1914 exceeded the army's capabilities, contributing to heavy early losses for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-conrad-von-hotzendorf.jpg" alt="Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing Collection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HOTZENDORF,_GEN._CONRAD_VON._GENERAL_STAFF,_GERMANY_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chief of Staff <b>Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf</b>'s offensive planning was badly at odds with the army's real capabilities: in 1914 the invasions of Serbia were repulsed at Cer and the Kolubara, while in Galicia the army was routed around Lemberg (Lviv), losing much of its trained peacetime officer…</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
